{"name":"Great Basin","hex":"#576e8b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Great Basin","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A large arid area of the western United States of America."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Great_Basin"}],"entries":[{"title":"Great Basin","blocks":[{"text":"The Great Basin is the largest area of contiguous endorheic watersheds, those with no outlets to the ocean, in North America. It spans nearly all of Nevada, much of Utah, and portions of California, Idaho, Oregon, Wyoming, and Baja California. It is noted for both its arid climate and the basin and range topography that varies from the North American low point at Badwater Basin in Death Valley to the highest point of the contiguous United States, less than 100 miles (160 km) away at the summit of Mount Whitney. The region spans several physiographic divisions, biomes, ecoregions, and deserts."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Great_Basin_map.gif/330px-Great_Basin_map.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Basin","wikidata":"Q966943"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Great_Basin_Ecoregions.gif/500px-Great_Basin_Ecoregions.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Great_Basin_Ecoregions.gif/1280px-Great_Basin_Ecoregions.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Great_Basin_Ecoregions.gif/500px-Great_Basin_Ecoregions.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Ecoregions as currently delineated by the Environmental Protection Agency[13] and World Wildlife Fund[14]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGreat_Basin_Ecoregions.gif"},{"srcset":"https: